      description: 'Choose toys for interest, motor access, communication and safety rather than relying on therapeutic labels.',
      lead: 'Accessible toys let a child take part in a way that interests them. A diagnosis and an age label alone cannot determine a good match.',
      keys: ['inclusive toys', 'toys for autistic children', 'motor access', 'toys for speech delay', 'playing with AAC'],
      evidence: ['The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends materials matched to children’s abilities and interests and values interaction around toys. An expensive product or an “educational” label does not automatically make a better choice. [toys]', 'Access involves reaching, manipulating, seeing, hearing and influencing play. ASHA considers motor, sensory and communication factors when selecting AAC resources; this perspective helps adults ask access questions without making every toy a therapeutic device. [aac]'],
      steps: ['Notice what the child seeks: movement, construction, caring for dolls or exploring pictures. Begin with an actual interest rather than a supposedly diagnosis-appropriate activity.', 'Check whether the toy can be reached, held and activated without an uncomfortable posture. Consider large, stable pieces and a suitable non-slip surface.', 'Check that the child can choose, refuse and request help. Keep their established communication system available alongside gestures and words.', 'Compare simple options: knob puzzles, easy-grip blocks or an instrument responding to light touch. Try before buying when possible.', 'Choose for observed participation and safety rather than the number of features. Seek competent professional advice for electrical adaptations or specialist access selection.'],
      example: 'Two children may enjoy the same puzzle with different access. One handles the pieces; another indicates a piece and its location for a partner to place it. Both can make decisions and participate. If direct handling is a separate goal, work on it without devaluing participation through choice.',
      adapt: 'For 2- and 3-year-olds, and older children who mouth objects, size and detachable pieces need particular attention. Follow safety age warnings. Do not offer an unsafe version because it appears to match a learning level. Materials should respect interests and the dignity of the child’s actual age.',
      record: 'Notice whether the child can start an action, choose to continue and finish. Pain, rapid fatigue or postural effort are reasons to review access. Hospital safety guidance highlights small parts, batteries and magnets; do not leave button batteries, loose magnets or damaged components accessible. [safety]',
      yes: ['A simple toy can allow choices, exploration and interaction.', 'Suitable access may involve an adaptation or another person’s cooperation.'],
      no: ['One toy is ideal for every child with the same diagnosis.', 'A therapeutic label guarantees clinical benefits or higher intelligence.'],
      faq: [['Which toys suit an autistic child?', 'Start with interests and sensory comfort, check access and safety, and explore without demanding one correct way to play.'], ['What are the best toys for speech delay?', 'No toy list treats speech delay. Objects that invite choosing, commenting and sharing may create communication opportunities with an available adult.'], ['Can a baby toy suit an older child?', 'Consider safety, interests and actual age. Look for a similar operating principle in an age-respectful presentation rather than reducing the child to a developmental age.'], ['Can an app replace physical toys?', 'No. Objects, movement and people have their own place in play. A digital resource can be complementary when accessible and meaningful.']],
      bridge: [['Communicating choices is part of access to play.', 'Communicate in Talk+ combines picture-and-voice cards when this format suits the child.'], ['Choosing technology also involves the quality of the experience.', 'Talk+ has no adverts and works offline; adults still need to check comfort and touch access.']],
